    "media:imagetext": "#9\na long siren followed by the beating on brass gongs around town at\nstrategic points, so even the soundly sleeping chauffers of waiting\nmotor cars are awakened.\nThe celling was about six thousand feet\nand above that a solid overcast.\nThree Jap planes came in from the\neast, flew directly over the city in a V formation.\nThey flew\ndirectly over the American Embassy and released their bombs soon\nafter crossing over.\nTheir target was the military air fields from\nwhich all airplanes have long since departed.\nThe hangars are already\na shambles and coolies repair the field\nas fast as the Japs can hit it.\nIne oninese at Hangkow, and possibly here, build wood and paper air-\nplanes which they park at random around the edges of the field. The\nJaps drop tons of bombs, costing thousands of dollars,\nand report the\ndestruction of say ten enemy planes.\nThat night the Chinese kite makers\nrebuild their paper planes, move them to new positions and the next day\nthe process is repeated. In this manner the Japanese have reported the\ndestruction of more airplanes than the Chinese ever owned. I suppose\nthe Japanese Foreign office will soon start issuing claims of \"non-co-\noperation\" and\"insincerity\" and threaten to \"take measures\".\nAs the planes went over the city the anti-aircrait guns cut\nloose from every side and machine guns rattled like the sound of driving\nrivets.\nThe anti-aircraft shells maûe black puffs of smoke in the gray\nsky arouna the planes, but none exploded near enough to do any damage\nand the planes nela their formation and their course seemingly undis-\nturbed by the noise chorus from below. The bombs were dropped inside\nine wall which encircles the city.\nhere in this room all lights are out and there is no heat.\nam writing next to a window, one of two such openings with a door between,\na small amount of light filters in Irom\nthe courtyard over the double row\no: sand bage piled celling high in front of the door and windows.\nroom is one set aside for meetings and conferences.\nIt is on the firgt\nfloor of the four storied magnificent Ministry of Communications building\nbuilt in modern steel and concrete to resemble the old Chinese architec-\nture. I am told that the floor above us is bomb-proof. I hope that\nstatement is true for the siren outside is at this moment screaming the\nsignal which warns that another raid is on the way.\nOutposts with\ntelephone connections to the siren stations are flung around Nanking in\na circle so that warnings are given about 15 minutes before the planes get\nhere.\nTo augment the siren a small boy walked through the corridor with\nan alarm clock ringing vociferously the while he wound it vigorously.\nLiterally an alarm clock!\nWe had our board meeting in the candle light -\nall lights are off during raids - and I'll confess that it was a bit eerie\nand disconcerting until we got the \"all clear\" signal, when lights were\nturned on and it was again a case of business as usual. The Ministry\nBuilding is checked as an objective on all the Jap maps which have been\ntaken from planes shot down.\nOur meeting was a total loss so far as\ngetting anything accomplished along lines desired by us.\nHad dinner at the Metropolitan Hotel with some of my American\naviation iriends\nall of whom are here trying to get \"their share\" (meaning\nIn each case all) of an order for seven million U.S.dollars worth of air-\nplanes, motors and parts.\nThey are all worried about deliveries and their\nschemes are legion for beating the Japanese blockade and various American\nrestrictive export regulations. The Board Room is a large square room with\n-9-",
